// Kiddo School — Matching & Sorting (Class 10 · age 2) · Learning Path lessons.
// One parent-led teaching lesson per flashcard, written fresh for two-year-olds:
// thinking happens out loud, pointing counts as an answer, and every game runs
// on things already in the house. Every "notice" block references only what the
// card's alt text verifies ("Two matching red balls for a toddler same-and-match
// activity" and friends — msLesson in src/lessons.mjs), so no artwork details
// beyond that are claimed. Library card pages in data-matching-and-sorting.mjs
// stay a separate WHAT/WHY experience — no text is shared with them.
export const lpContent={
'same-balls':{
 title:'Same Balls',one:'Same is among the first thinking words a toddler owns, and two identical balls are the gentlest place on earth to try it out.',
 seoTitle:'Same Balls Lesson for 2-Year-Olds — How to Teach Matching and Sorting (Class 10)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for 2-year-olds: find the pair that matches, say same together, and turn every doubled thing in the house into a matching game.',
 intro:'This lesson opens the thinking deck with its easiest question: two balls, exactly alike, waiting to be spotted. Your toddler points, you say the word, and same begins its long career.',
 what:'Matching means noticing that two things are alike in the way that matters — here, two balls that look like twins. Nothing to add, nothing to fix; the whole job is seeing it.',
 mean:'At two, thinking is done with eyes and hands before it is done with words. Same is a comparison, and comparisons are the quiet start of maths: your toddler is sorting the world into this one and that one.',
 know:'Any answer counts: a point at each ball, a tap on both, a shouted same, or a happy grab. If one ball gets all the attention today, say same for both and move on — the word lands through repetition, not through tests.',
 why:'Twins live all over the house: two socks, two spoons, two shoes. A word with that many hiding places gets practised all day without a single extra minute of lesson time.',
 dev:'Visual discrimination (spotting alike and not alike), early comparison language, and the first stirrings of sorting — the root every matching and maths skill grows from.',
 vocab:'same, match, two, balls, pair, twin',
 teach:'1) Put the card flat between you, run one finger from ball to ball, and say the story simply: two balls — same.\n2) Touch each ball in turn as you say ball, so the count and the match happen under your fingertip where your toddler can see.\n3) Find one real pair in the room — two spoons, two blocks — and lay them beside the card: same again! Same everywhere.\n4) Let your toddler lead the second round: whatever they point at, name it and add same if it matches, even if the match is only a rough one.\n5) Leave the card out where the day can find it, and say same warmly whenever a natural pair turns up — socks at dressing time are tomorrow’s lesson.',
 say:'"Two balls! Look — this one, and this one. They match. Same! Can you touch both balls? Same… and same. Let’s find two more that match somewhere in this room."',
 notice:'The card shows two matching red balls for a same-and-match activity — one red pair, nothing else on the picture to distract the eye. Ask your toddler to touch each ball, then to touch both at once with one hand.',
 ask:'"Can you point to both balls?" "Are they the same?" "Can you find two spoons that match?" "Where is another pair hiding?"',
 childSay:'A same shouted like a discovery, a solemn point at each ball, or both balls grabbed in one fist — every version is the thinking showing itself. Some toddlers answer by fetching a second object to match the first; that is the idea getting up and walking.',
 real:'Dressing time is a matching rally: two socks, two shoes, two mittens. Hand over one and let the hunt for its twin happen on your toddler’s terms — every found pair is the word same doing its job.',
 play:'Pair hunt: scatter four objects — two that match, two that do not — and let your toddler hand you the matching pair. Keep the objects big, the differences obvious, and the applause generous.',
 check:'Hold the card up tomorrow and ask which two match. An instant point means the idea stuck; a wander first is fine too, because the wandering is the comparing. Two taps on both balls is a pass with honours.',
 tips:'Say same about yourself too — same cup, same chair, same song again — so the word stretches past this one card. If your toddler matches things that are only roughly alike, accept it warmly: categories start loose and tighten on their own.',
 safety:'Keep every matching object bigger than a toilet-roll tube — at two, anything small enough to swallow ends up inspected by mouth. The game lives on the floor, away from stairs and hot drinks.'},
'same-apples':{
 title:'Same Apples',one:'Two red apples, alike as two peas — and snack time is about to become a matching lesson your toddler can actually eat.',
 seoTitle:'Same Apples Lesson for 2-Year-Olds — How to Teach Matching and Sorting (Class 10)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for 2-year-olds: match the apple pair on the card, then lay out real apple slices and let matching become snack time.',
 intro:'This lesson pairs the card with the fruit bowl: two apples alike on the picture, two apple slices alike on the plate, and one word — same — doing all the work.',
 what:'An apple is round, red and familiar — most toddlers have held one long before they point at one. Two that match make the sameness impossible to miss.',
 mean:'Familiar objects make matching easier: a toddler spots twin apples faster than twin shapes because the apple is already an old friend. Familiarity is not cheating; it is the ladder.',
 know:'The match does not need naming — a glance travelling back and forth between the two apples is already comparing. One apple getting eaten mid-game turns into a bonus lesson: one, and then one.',
 why:'Apples show up daily, so this card reruns itself at snack time all week. Food games hold a hungry toddler’s attention like nothing else in the cupboard.',
 dev:'Matching familiar objects, back-and-forth visual comparing, and early counting-to-two woven into a snack — thinking skills delivered with lunch.',
 vocab:'apples, same, match, two, red, snack',
 teach:'1) Show the card and name the pair slowly: apple… apple. Pause on the second one so your toddler’s eyes make the trip across.\n2) Ask where the two apples are and follow your toddler’s finger — touch each one as it is found, saying apple, same.\n3) Bring the real thing: two apple slices on a plate, lined up like the picture. Say the word again over the real pair: same!\n4) Let your toddler eat one — then narrate the change: one apple… gone. What is left? One. Matching survives even a missing apple.\n5) Rerun the game at snack time through the week: two crackers, two banana rounds, two of whatever is already on the plate.',
 say:'"Two apples, and they match! Apple and apple — same. Here are your apple slices, lined up just like the picture. Same, same! Which one goes first?"',
 notice:'The card shows two matching red apples — a pair, drawn alike from stem to bottom. Ask your toddler to touch the first apple and then its twin, and to point at anything the two share.',
 ask:'"Where are the two apples?" "Do they match?" "Can you find the same ones on your plate?" "Which apple goes first?"',
 childSay:'Matching at two is mostly looking: a gaze that travels from one apple to the other and back is a working comparison. Words that may arrive: same, apple, or a decisive "dis one!" with a slice already in hand.',
 real:'The fruit bowl is this card’s storeroom. Next shop, let your toddler drop pairs into the bag — two apples, two bananas — and say same at every pair that lands.',
 play:'Snack twins: put two of a food and one odd item on a plate, and let your toddler pick out the pair. Crackers, cheese cubes, cucumber coins — the pair is the prize, and also the pudding.',
 check:'Offer two objects that match and one that does not, and ask for the matching two. A toddler who gathers the twins has the idea; a toddler who lines up all three has the idea too, just at sorting speed.',
 tips:'Eat one of your matched pair on purpose and say what happened — the gone moment teaches one and empty for free. Keep the word same attached to pairs only, so it stays precise.',
 safety:'Cut apple slices thin and soft for two-year-old teeth, and stay close while the chewing happens — firm fruit is real work for small mouths. Whole grapes and whole nuts stay off the plate entirely.'},
'same-cups':{
 title:'Same Cups',one:'Two blue cups that match — the kind your toddler clinks and stacks already, now promoted to a proper thinking game.',
 seoTitle:'Same Cups Lesson for 2-Year-Olds — How to Teach Matching and Sorting (Class 10)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for 2-year-olds: spot the twin cups on the card, then reunite cup pairs from the cupboard in a game that tidies as it teaches.',
 intro:'This lesson moves matching into the kitchen, where the cups live: find the pair on the card, then find the pairs in the cupboard and reunite them with a satisfying clink.',
 what:'Cups are everyday equipment your toddler already handles — which makes two of them the perfect matching material. Same shape, same size, same job: the match is everywhere you look.',
 mean:'Matching objects your toddler has handled a hundred times is easier than matching pictures alone, and easy wins are what build a thinking habit. The cupboard is the second deck.',
 know:'The clink is the reward: two cups reunited get to touch, tap and stack. If your toddler matches a cup with a lid instead, say what they found — cup and lid go together too — and steer gently back to twins.',
 why:'Unloading a cupboard is a matching rally hiding inside a chore. One word — same — turns helping into the lesson and the lesson into helping.',
 dev:'Matching by sight and by hand, hiding-and-reuniting play, and real helping — the confidence that comes from doing a grown-up job with grown-up things.',
 vocab:'cups, same, match, pair, blue, clink',
 teach:'1) Show the card and let your toddler’s eyes find the pair: two cups — same. Say it once, then wait.\n2) Ask your toddler to touch each cup in turn, and add the sound effect: tap, tap. Two taps make a match.\n3) Open the cupboard and hunt real twins together: two cups stood side by side, lifted, clinked gently, and lined up in a matching row.\n4) Play the reunion game: mix two cup pairs up, hand your toddler one cup, and let them find its partner wherever it landed.\n5) Fold the word into the day: same cups at dinner, same cups at bath, until your toddler starts spotting matches before you do.',
 say:'"Two cups — same! Can you tap them together? Clink! Now let’s find the cups in the cupboard. Two the same… here is one, where is its friend? There! Clink — a match."',
 notice:'The card shows two matching blue cups — same colour, same shape, drawn as a pair. Ask your toddler to tap each cup on the card, then hold up two fingers: two cups, one match.',
 ask:'"Where are the two cups?" "Can you tap them together?" "Can you find two cups in the cupboard?" "Is this one the same?"',
 childSay:'Expect a clink, a stack, or both cups in one determined grip. A toddler who says same at the clink is ahead; a toddler who just keeps clinking is doing the exact repetition the word needs.',
 real:'Every cupboard, drawer and laundry basket holds pairs. Cup matching after shopping, lid matching after washing up — the card idea keeps working in the corners of the day.',
 play:'Cup orchestra: three pairs of cups, mixed up on the floor. Your toddler reunites the pairs; every reunion earns its clink. Two minutes of noise, one tidy cupboard, zero new toys needed.',
 check:'Next time two matching cups are within reach, ask for the two that match. A correct grab, a correct point, or a clink started without prompting all pass — the clink most of all.',
 tips:'Plastic only for this game: clinking should be loud, not breakable. If the cupboard itself is the attraction, that is fine — matching cups on a real shelf beats any worksheet ever printed.',
 safety:'Choose lightweight cups your toddler can lift without a crash landing on the toes, and keep the game off hard floors where everyone crawls. Grown-up hands open the cupboard; small hands do the matching.'},
'different-apple-banana':{
 title:'Apple and Banana',one:'Two fruit, clearly not twins — this card teaches the other half of thinking: not just same, but different.',
 seoTitle:'Apple and Banana Lesson for 2-Year-Olds — How to Teach Matching and Sorting (Class 10)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for 2-year-olds: compare an apple and a banana side by side, name what makes them different, and try the game at snack time.',
 intro:'This lesson flips the deck’s first question: after finding twins, your toddler now spots the pair that refuses to match — and the word for that is different.',
 what:'An apple is round; a banana is long and curvy. Two fruit, two shapes, two colours — sitting side by side, they make different visible from across the room.',
 mean:'Different only means something next to a same: your toddler needs both words to feel the contrast. Comparing pairs like this is the seed of describing, sorting and, eventually, explaining choices.',
 know:'Any noticed difference counts — size, colour, shape, or simply pointing at each in turn and waiting. You supply the words: long, round, yellow, red. Your toddler supplies the looking.',
 why:'Fruit arrives daily, so the comparing can rerun at every meal without effort. Best of all, the difference is harmless: nobody has to be right, because both fruit are staying.',
 dev:'Comparing two familiar objects, difference-spotting, and describing words (long, round, colours) — the vocabulary of noticing, built on snack food.',
 vocab:'apple, banana, different, long, round, fruit',
 teach:'1) Lay the card between you and name each fruit in turn: an apple… a banana. Two names, two points, one breath between them.\n2) Say the contrast out loud and let your hands show it: the apple is round, the banana is long. Curve your arms for the banana, circle them for the apple.\n3) Ask the question both ways: are they the same? No — different! Let your toddler hear the word land right after the same.\n4) Bring both real fruit to the table and compare for real: hold them side by side, roll the apple, try to roll the banana, and talk about what each one did.\n5) Rerun at snack time with the words attached: the long one, the round one. If your toddler picks one, say why it was wanted — that is the description skill sprouting.',
 say:'"An apple and a banana! Are they the same? Look… no! The apple is round, the banana is long. Different! Can you show me long? Now round?"',
 notice:'The card shows an apple and a banana side by side for a same-and-different activity — two fruit, plainly not twins. Ask your toddler to point at the round one, then the long one.',
 ask:'"Are they the same or different?" "Which one is long?" "Which one is round?" "Which one do you want to eat?"',
 childSay:'Different is a harder word than same, so expect pointing first: one hand on the apple, one on the banana. Words that may come: nana, appo, or a confident different borrowed straight from your voice.',
 real:'The fruit bowl runs this lesson all week: every snack is a chance to hand over the long one or the round one and let the choice happen. Shopping trips work too — same shelf, real fruit, live comparing.',
 play:'Fruit roll test: an apple, a banana, a low rug. Which one rolls? The apple obeys, the banana wobbles off course — and the difference becomes something your toddler’s hands have tried.',
 check:'Next snack, hold up an apple and a banana and ask which is which. A correct point passes; a grabbed banana with a satisfied nod passes better, because choosing means the difference is being used.',
 tips:'Keep the pair friendly and the quiz absent: nobody loses for mixing up a nana and an appo. Say each word cleanly once, then hand over the fruit — the mouth follows the taste.',
 safety:'Slice the apple for toddler teeth and peel the banana all the way back; stay close for the chewing. The roll test stays on the floor, away from table edges and other diners’ breakfasts.'},
'different-animals':{
 title:'Cat and Dog',one:'A cat and a dog, sitting side by side and refusing to match — the most familiar different pair a toddler owns.',
 seoTitle:'Cat and Dog Lesson for 2-Year-Olds — How to Teach Matching and Sorting (Class 10)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for 2-year-olds: look at the cat and dog, name what makes them different, and let sounds do the comparing out loud.',
 intro:'This lesson borrows the two animals toddlers know best and asks the deck’s favourite question about them: same or different? Sounds, tails and sizes all count as answers.',
 what:'A cat and a dog are both animals, both furry, both pets — and still clearly not the same. That near-miss is what makes the pair so good: alike enough to compare, different enough to see.',
 mean:'Differences a toddler can hear are the easiest to feel: one animal says woof, the other says meow. Sound differences arrive before word differences, and both count as real comparing.',
 know:'The question is open on purpose — feet, sounds, size, tails, whatever your toddler notices is the right answer. Your job is to supply the words, not the verdict.',
 why:'Cats and dogs fill a toddler’s week — out the window, in books, at grandma’s. A different-pair with daily reruns builds itself without another lesson minute.',
 dev:'Comparing two familiar animals, sound-to-picture linking, and open describing — the skill the animal-sound cards build, now pointed at contrast instead of naming.',
 vocab:'cat, dog, different, woof, meow, tail',
 teach:'1) Show the card and greet both animals by name: a cat and a dog. One point each, one name each.\n2) Ask the deck’s question: are they the same? Then answer together, slowly: no — different!\n3) Hand each animal its voice: the dog says woof, the cat says meow. Take turns making both sounds — the difference your toddler can hear is the strongest one there is.\n4) Compare out loud in body language: the dog is big, the cat is small; the dog has a long tail, the cat has a curlier one. Point at each as you say it.\n5) Rerun the pair in the wild: a dog on the street, a cat on a wall, or both in a book at bedtime — different, every time they meet.',
 say:'"A cat and a dog! Same or different? Look closely… different! The dog says woof. The cat says meow. Can you make both sounds? Big dog… little cat — different animals."',
 notice:'The card shows a cat and a dog together for a same-and-different activity — two animals sitting side by side, not twins by any stretch. Ask your toddler to point at the cat, then the dog, then both at once.',
 ask:'"Are they the same or different?" "Where is the cat?" "What does the dog say?" "Can you make the cat sound?"',
 childSay:'Woof and meow in the same breath is a full answer — the sounds prove the comparing happened. Some toddlers answer with their whole body: big stomps for dog, soft creeping for cat.',
 real:'Walks and windows supply the reruns: every real cat and dog your toddler meets is the card come alive, twice over. Point, name, compare in one short sentence, and walk on.',
 play:'Big dog, little cat: you are the dog with big stomps and loud woofs; your toddler is the cat, creeping quiet with tiny meows. Swap roles after two rounds and feel the difference in your own legs.',
 check:'Show the card later and ask the question cold: same or different? A clear different, a point at each animal, or both sounds in order all pass. If only one animal gets named, that is a start, not a miss.',
 tips:'Let the sounds carry the lesson — contrast heard is contrast learned at two. If your toddler calls both animals dog, agree the category warmly (yes, animals!) and slip the word cat in once more, without fuss.',
 safety:'Real animals follow real rules: ask the owner before anyone pets, and watch from your hip until the answer comes. The comparing happens with words; the touching waits for permission.'},
'different-shapes':{
 title:'Circle and Triangle',one:'A circle and a triangle side by side — one is round, one has corners, and the difference is something your toddler can trace.',
 seoTitle:'Circle and Triangle Lesson for 2-Year-Olds — How to Teach Matching and Sorting (Class 10)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for 2-year-olds: compare a circle and a triangle, trace the difference with one finger, and hunt round and cornered things at home.',
 intro:'This lesson finishes the different half of the deck with shapes: a smooth circle and a pointed triangle, and a difference small hands can feel as well as see.',
 what:'A circle is round all the way around, with no corners; a triangle has three straight sides and three points. Drawn side by side, the pair makes different easy to trace.',
 mean:'Shape differences are best learned by touch and motion: a finger travelling around a circle never stops, and a finger on a triangle turns three times. The hands file the difference where words will later live.',
 know:'The words matter less than the noticing: point, trace, and let your toddler travel the edges with one finger. Rolling is the circle’s party trick — let a ball prove it.',
 why:'Round and pointy divide the whole toy basket, the kitchen and the street. A difference this findable reruns itself every playtime without a single card in view.',
 dev:'Shape discrimination by sight and touch, corner-and-curve vocabulary, and finger tracing — the pre-writing skill hiding inside a matching card.',
 vocab:'circle, triangle, round, sides, corners, different',
 teach:'1) Show the card and travel it with your finger: all the way around the circle — round and round, no stops. Then walk the triangle’s three sides, stopping at each corner.\n2) Say the difference simply: the circle is round; the triangle has corners. Let one finger prove each claim.\n3) Hand your toddler the finger-trace turn and count the corners together out loud: one, two, three.\n4) Bring the rolling test: a ball rolls, a block refuses. The difference just became something your toddler pushed with their own hands.\n5) Hunt the pair through the room: round plates, round lids, pointy book corners, triangle roof ends. Round or corners — different, every find.',
 say:'"A circle and a triangle — different! My finger goes round and round the circle… no corners! The triangle goes one, two, three — corners! Can you trace them? Round… and pointy."',
 notice:'The card shows a circle and a triangle for a different-shapes activity — one round shape, one pointed shape, side by side. Ask your toddler to trace the circle’s edge, then count the triangle’s corners with a fingertip.',
 ask:'"Which one is round?" "Where are the corners?" "Can you trace the circle?" "Does the triangle roll?"',
 childSay:'A tracing finger is the answer at this age, especially one that slows down at the corners. Words that may arrive: round for sure, sometimes tri-gul for the triangle — every attempt gets an echo, not a correction.',
 real:'Round and pointy fill the day: wheels, plates, buttons against book corners, roof tips, sandwich halves. Name the shape of one real thing per outing and the card keeps teaching from the drawer.',
 play:'Sort the basket: one bowl for round things, one for pointy things, five mixed objects. Your toddler drops each find and announces — with hands if not words — which world it belongs to.',
 check:'Hold the card up cold tomorrow and ask which one is round. A confident point, a rolling gesture, or a finger that starts travelling the edge all pass. Two named shapes is bonus territory at two.',
 tips:'Trace before you name: the finger teaches the shape, the word just files it. If your toddler calls every corner triangle, accept the enthusiasm and offer the right shape once — accuracy grows from interest, not pressure.',
 safety:'The rolling test stays on the floor: a ball released indoors travels faster than any two-year-old expects. Keep the shape hunt away from genuinely sharp corners — card corners, not table corners, are for pointing.'},
'match-red':{
 title:'Match the Red One',one:'One red thing up top, a row of choices below — find its red twin, and a colour hunt turns into a thinking win.',
 seoTitle:'Match the Red One Lesson for 2-Year-Olds — How to Teach Matching and Sorting (Class 10)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for 2-year-olds: match red to red on the card, then run the same find-the-red game with real objects in one room.',
 intro:'This lesson turns matching into a colour game: one red thing above, a small row below, and exactly one answer your toddler gets to find and claim.',
 what:'Matching by colour means ignoring shape and size and finding the one that shares the colour. Here the colour is red — loud, common and usually a toddler’s first colour word.',
 mean:'Colour matching is harder than object matching: your toddler must decide what matters (the colour) and what does not (everything else). That filtering is real thinking, dressed as a game.',
 know:'The hunt matters more than the hit: a wrong choice gets a kind look and a together-check, never a fix. Red said back to you in any accent counts as the answer.',
 why:'Red is everywhere — coats, cups, cars, berries — so the game reruns all day. Every found red thing is a repetition of both the colour and the matching skill.',
 dev:'Colour recognition, visual scanning down a row of choices, and decision-making — your toddler choosing one answer and committing to it with a point.',
 vocab:'red, match, find, same, colour, look',
 teach:'1) Show the card and name the top red thing, then sweep your finger down the row below: one of these matches. Let the searching start.\n2) Ask the question simply: can you find the other red one? Then wait — count five slow breaths before you help.\n3) When the red twin gets found, trace from top to bottom with one finger: red… red. Same! The connection made visible.\n4) If a wrong choice comes first, look together kindly: this one is blue — where is red? Then let your toddler take the next pick themselves.\n5) Rerun with real objects: put three things on the table, two red and one not, and let the same find happen in three dimensions.',
 say:'"Here is the red one. Can you find the other red one, down below? Look at each one… is it red? That one! Red and red — they match! Say it with me: red!"',
 notice:'The card shows a red colour matching activity with visual choices — one red to match and a row to search. Ask your toddler to point at the red up top first, then hunt the row below.',
 ask:'"Can you find the other red one?" "Is this one red?" "Where else is red in this room?" "What did you match?"',
 childSay:'A decisive point is the usual answer, often with a shout: red! or dat one! Some toddlers match with their whole arm swept across the row — the sweep is scanning, and scanning is the skill.',
 real:'Street walks turn red-hunt: post boxes, stop signs, red coats, red cars. One found red per outing keeps the lesson alive without a card in sight.',
 play:'Red picnic: gather three red things and two others from one room onto a blanket, and let your toddler hand over only the red ones. Wrong hand-overs get named and thanked, not corrected.',
 check:'Tomorrow, two red things and one blue: ask for the red pair. A grab of both reds passes; a pause at the blue with a shake of the head passes even better, because not-red is the deeper half of the idea.',
 tips:'Keep the row short — three or four choices, not ten. Say red before each find and after it: red cup, red found. The word before the point is what builds the label, not just the lucky tap.',
 safety:'Table-top finds stay bigger than a toilet-roll tube and away from the mouth — red berries found on the ground are for birds, not toddlers. The blanket game lives on the floor, clear of stairs.'},
'match-blue':{
 title:'Match the Blue One',one:'The matching game again, dressed in blue — a calm colour, a fresh row, and one twin waiting to be found.',
 seoTitle:'Match the Blue One Lesson for 2-Year-Olds — How to Teach Matching and Sorting (Class 10)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for 2-year-olds: match blue to blue, run a blue hunt through the room, and enjoy being wrong on purpose so your toddler can fix you.',
 intro:'This lesson reruns the matching game in a new colour, because at two, repetition with one change is exactly how a skill sets: same game, blue answer, growing confidence.',
 what:'Blue is a calm, common colour — sky-coloured, easy on the eye, and hiding in most rooms. The card gives one blue to match and a short row to search.',
 mean:'Second runs are where learning shows: your toddler has matched red once and now gets to prove the skill travels. A different colour with the same rule is the gentlest possible stretch.',
 know:'Let your toddler lead the search and you follow the finger. The best twist of this lesson: you pick a wrong answer on purpose, look stumped, and let your proud teacher correct you.',
 why:'Blue fills rooms quietly — cups, books, socks, the sky out the window. A findable colour keeps the matching game in free supply all week.',
 dev:'Colour matching on a second colour, scanning a row of choices, and the confidence leap of correcting a grown-up — teaching is the deepest form of knowing.',
 vocab:'blue, match, find, same, sky, look',
 teach:'1) Show the card and find the blue up top together, then sweep the row below with one finger: which one matches?\n2) Let your toddler search alone this time — the game is familiar now, and second rounds belong to the child.\n3) When the blue twin is found, say the pairing out loud: blue and blue — match! Then run the whole find again, start to finish, with your toddler driving.\n4) Play the wrong-point trick: you pick a wrong one, frown gently, and ask is this blue? Wait for the correction — being the teacher is a powerful feeling at two.\n5) Finish with a real blue hunt in one room: three blue finds, named and touched, each one a match for the card.',
 say:'"Here is blue. Can you find the blue twin below? Look… look… there it is! Blue and blue — same! Now watch me… this one? No? Show me the blue. You found it!"',
 notice:'The card shows a blue colour matching activity with visual choices — one blue to match above, a row to search below. Ask your toddler to point at the blue up top, then find where blue happens again.',
 ask:'"Can you find the blue one?" "Is this blue?" "Where is blue in this room?" "Did I pick the right one?"',
 childSay:'A quick point, a grabbed card, or a triumphant no! when you point wrong — the correction is the highest form of this lesson. Blue said as boo or bwoo is the word arriving right on time.',
 real:'Blue lives outdoors too: the sky is the biggest blue your toddler will ever match, free of charge twice a day. Point up, say blue, and the card’s game is running in the clouds.',
 play:'Fix the grown-up: lay out three objects, two blue and one not, and deliberately pick up the wrong one yourself. Your toddler reaches to swap it — teaching you, and proving the colour, in one move.',
 check:'Ask for blue from across the room: fetch something blue! A correct fetch passes outright. If two blues get fetched, say both names warmly — the category is growing faster than the lesson planned.',
 tips:'One deliberate mistake per game, not more: the trick works because it is rare. If your toddler starts wrong-pointing you back, the game has fully landed — take turns being wrong.',
 safety:'Room hunts stay at toddler height — blue on high shelves waits for a grown-up helper. Nothing small enough to swallow joins the game, however blue and tempting it looks.'},
'match-circle':{
 title:'Match the Circle',one:'One circle up top, shapes below — the match your finger can trace before your toddler’s words can name it.',
 seoTitle:'Match the Circle Lesson for 2-Year-Olds — How to Teach Matching and Sorting (Class 10)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for 2-year-olds: trace the circle, match it to its twin among the choices, and let one walking finger do the thinking.',
 intro:'This lesson moves matching into shapes: one circle waits above, a row of shapes sits below, and a travelling finger is all the tool the job needs.',
 what:'A circle is the shape with no corners and no stops — one smooth edge, all the way around. It is the first shape most toddlers recognise and the easiest to match by feel.',
 mean:'Tracing turns looking into knowing: a finger that walks the edge of a circle is measuring it. Shape matching by trace comes weeks before shape naming, and both are welcome.',
 know:'The answer is a point, a tap, or a finger that lands on the twin and stays. If other shapes get toured first, that is not wandering — that is comparing, and it is exactly the point.',
 why:'Circles run the world: plates, lids, wheels, buttons, the sun in every drawing. A matched circle is a shape skill your toddler can rehearse at every meal.',
 dev:'Shape matching, edge-tracing with one finger, and visual search through near-miss shapes — the close cousins that make the real match satisfying.',
 vocab:'circle, round, match, trace, edge, find',
 teach:'1) Show the card and trace the top circle with one slow finger, saying the journey out loud: round and round, no corners, no stops.\n2) Sweep the row below together, tracing each shape briefly: this one has corners… this one has corners… where is the circle?\n3) Let your toddler take the tracing turn — their finger, their pace, your quiet company. The match usually lands when the finger lands.\n4) Confirm the match with a double trace: top circle, bottom circle, same journey twice. Say it simply: circle and circle — match.\n5) Hunt one real circle before the lesson ends: a plate, a lid, a wheel — traced too, so the shape leaves the card and enters the room.',
 say:'"A circle! Round and round and round — no corners. Can you find the circle below? Trace it with me… round and round. Same! Two circles, a matching pair."',
 notice:'The card shows a circle matching activity with basic shape choices — one circle to match and other shapes nearby. Ask your toddler to trace the big circle first, then find which shape below shares its round edge.',
 ask:'"Can you trace the circle?" "Where is the other circle?" "Does this one have corners?" "Can you find a round plate?"',
 childSay:'Expect a tracing finger more than a word: a slow trip around the chosen shape is the answer in motion. Round may arrive as roun or wah-wah — the shape is clearly known even when the word is not.',
 real:'Round things beg to be traced: the rim of a cup, the edge of a bowl, the face in the mirror. One traced circle a day keeps this shape in working memory all week.',
 play:'Circle dinner: before the meal, trace one round thing on the table together — plate, rim, lid — and say circle. Three days of this and your toddler will start the tracing without you.',
 check:'Show the card cold and ask for the circle’s twin. A direct point passes; a point that starts at a square then corrects itself passes better — the comparison ran and the answer improved.',
 tips:'The finger is the teacher here — keep your own finger slow and your voice slower. If two shapes get chosen cheerfully, trace both and let the edges disagree: corners or no corners, the circle wins.',
 safety:'No safety angle beyond the usual: this is a sitting, tracing, table-top game. Keep cups of tea on the far side of the table — the only circles a two-year-old should not trace are the hot ones.'},
'match-triangle':{
 title:'Match the Triangle',one:'Three sides, three corners, one twin below — the shape match that ends in counting taps on points together.',
 seoTitle:'Match the Triangle Lesson for 2-Year-Olds — How to Teach Matching and Sorting (Class 10)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for 2-year-olds: count a triangle’s three corners, match it to its twin below, and spot pointy shapes around the house.',
 intro:'This lesson closes the shape-matching run with the pointiest card in the deck: one triangle above, its twin hiding below, and three corners to count on the way.',
 what:'A triangle is the shape with three straight sides and three sharp corners — the one that looks like a roof or a mountain. Among softer shapes, those points shine out.',
 mean:'Counting the corners gives the match a handle a two-year-old can grip: one, two, three taps on the points. Numbers and shapes meet on this card, and both get stronger.',
 know:'The trace-and-count trip is the real work: side, corner, side, corner. Whether the count lands on two or three matters less than the pointing finger keeping its own rhythm.',
 why:'Triangles hide in plain sight — roofs, sandwiches cut corner to corner, mountains in books, ramps of toy tracks. Once named, they get spotted everywhere by proud new eyes.',
 dev:'Shape matching with counting woven in, corner-spotting, and finger tracing with stops — the precise finger control that later steadies a pencil.',
 vocab:'triangle, three, corners, sides, match, pointy',
 teach:'1) Show the card and trace the triangle slowly, stopping hard at each corner with a tap: one, two, three!\n2) Say the shape’s story simply: three sides, three corners, very pointy. Let your finger do the proving.\n3) Tour the row below together, tapping corners where they appear, until the twin triangle turns up — then trace both, corner to corner.\n4) Count the corners on your toddler’s fingers too: three corners, three fingers held up. The number and the shape now share a home.\n5) End with a real find: a triangle slice of toast, a roof out the window, a pointy mountain in a book — traced, tapped and counted once each.',
 say:'"A triangle! One, two, three corners — tap, tap, tap. Can you find the triangle below? Trace the sides… tap each point… there! Triangle and triangle — they match."',
 notice:'The card shows a triangle matching activity with basic shape choices — one triangle to match, other shapes beside it. Ask your toddler to tap each corner of the big triangle, then hunt the row for the shape with three points.',
 ask:'"How many corners?" "Where is the other triangle?" "Can you tap the points?" "Can you see a triangle out the window?"',
 childSay:'Corner-taps with a counting voice — boo, doo, free or otherwise — are a complete answer. A toddler who holds up three wobbly fingers is ahead of the lesson; a toddler who just taps the points is right on it.',
 real:'Lunchtime supplies triangles free of charge: toast and sandwiches cut corner to corner, pizza by the slice. Tap the corners, count them, then eat the lesson — the best cards are edible.',
 play:'Corner hunt: walk one room together tapping every pointy corner you are allowed to touch — book corners, box corners, cushion corners. Three taps per corner, then find the shape that has exactly that many.',
 check:'Ask cold tomorrow: where is the triangle? A point straight to the points passes. If the circle gets picked first, go back to the corner-tap together — the count always settles the argument.',
 tips:'Tap the corners louder than you say the words: the beat is what makes three memorable. Save this card for after the circle lesson — corners are easier to spot once round is known.',
 safety:'Pointy-hunt rules: cards and toast corners, not table corners or sharp edges — grown-up fingers check each target first. Sitting games stay on the floor, away from busy doorways.'},
'sort-by-color':{
 title:'Sorting by Color',one:'A pile, two colours, one job: reds here, blues there — the first true sorting work in the whole deck.',
 seoTitle:'Sorting by Color Lesson for 2-Year-Olds — How to Teach Matching and Sorting (Class 10)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for 2-year-olds: sort red and blue into two piles, name each group as it grows, and let tidying time become thinking time.',
 intro:'This lesson upgrades matching to sorting: instead of one twin, a whole pile splits into two teams, and your toddler puts every piece on its right side.',
 what:'Sorting means giving every object a home by rule — here, the rule is colour. Red things gather in one place, blue things in the other, and the muddle turns into order.',
 mean:'Sorting is matching grown up: your toddler checks each object against the rule again and again, holding the colour in mind between picks. That held rule is working memory, the quiet engine of all thinking.',
 know:'Two piles are plenty at this age — red and blue covers the whole job. Expect strays and stumbles: any object placed with a choice behind it counts, and the rule gets stronger each round.',
 why:'Sorting hides inside real life: laundry by person, shopping by cupboard, toys by box. A toddler who sorts red and blue on the card is one step from sorting spoons for real.',
 dev:'Rule-following, working memory across several picks, group naming, and tidy-up habits — the executive-function workout dressed as play.',
 vocab:'sort, red, blue, pile, group, together',
 teach:'1) Show the card and set the rule out loud, with your hands dividing the air: reds on this side, blues on that side.\n2) Sort the first two pieces yourself, narrating each: this is red — red pile. This is blue — blue pile. The rule made visible, twice.\n3) Hand your toddler the next piece and wait. Whatever they decide, say the choice back: red — yes! Or: blue again — are you sure? Then carry on.\n4) Name the piles as they grow: look at all the reds together, all the blues together. Groups deserve their names out loud.\n5) Finish by mixing it all up — a good scandal — and let the sorting run again, faster this time, with your toddler taking more of the picks.',
 say:'"Time to sort! Reds here, blues there. This one? Red — here it goes. Your turn… where does this one live? The red pile! Now all the reds are together, and all the blues. Sorted!"',
 notice:'The card shows red and blue objects for a sorting-by-color activity — one mixed pile waiting for two teams. Ask your toddler to point at a red one, then a blue one, before any sorting starts.',
 ask:'"Where do the reds go?" "Is this one red or blue?" "Can you put it with its colour?" "Where are all the blues?"',
 childSay:'A two-pile sort sounds like this: silence, a hover, a drop, then a look up for a verdict. Verdicts should be warm and rare — the sorting face is concentrating, and concentration needs air.',
 real:'Laundry is sorting with socks: this pile yours, that pile mine. Shopping unpacks by cupboard; bath toys by box. Two real sorts a day and the card keeps itself company on the shelf.',
 play:'Bowl sort: two bowls, one mixed handful of big blocks or socks. Reds bowl, blues bowl — then swap the rule suddenly, with happy gasps: now sort by size! A new rule on old pieces is the stretch that grows.',
 check:'Tip two reds and two blues into a line and ask for all the reds together. Four correct drops pass; two correct drops with two hovering seconds pass better, because the hovering is the thinking made visible.',
 tips:'Say the rule at the start of every round — sorting today, reds and blues — because the rule is the lesson, not the pieces. If a red lands in the blue pile without worry, hand it back gently: where do the reds live?',
 safety:'Sort with pieces too big to swallow and bowls too soft to bruise — the tidier the pieces, the easier the tidying. Floor sort, carpet sort, away from stair edges and underfoot grown-ups.'},
'sort-by-shape':{
 title:'Sorting by Shape',one:'The same pile, a brand-new rule: this time circles and squares split up — sorting twice proves the thinking.',
 seoTitle:'Sorting by Shape Lesson for 2-Year-Olds — How to Teach Matching and Sorting (Class 10)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for 2-year-olds: sort circles and squares into teams, switch the rule out loud, and let one toy box do two lessons.',
 intro:'This lesson runs the sort a second way: the objects stay, the rule changes from colour to shape, and your toddler discovers one pile can be split two different ways.',
 what:'Sorting by shape means the question changes: not which colour, but which outline. Circles gather with circles, squares with squares, and every piece gets asked the new question.',
 mean:'Rule-switching is the real stretch: the pieces your toddler sorted by colour must be re-asked, re-checked and re-homed today. Discovering that one pile can split two different ways is a big idea wearing a small game.',
 know:'Announce the new rule at the start of every round — shapes today! — because the rule is the whole lesson. Wrong drops are information: name what the piece is and let it try again.',
 why:'One toy box, two lessons: the same blocks that sorted by colour now sort by shape, doubling the play value of toys you already own. No new pieces required, ever.',
 dev:'Rule-switching, shape recognition under pressure, flexible thinking — and the early metaskill of knowing that sorting depends on the question you ask.',
 vocab:'shape, circles, squares, sort, rule, same',
 teach:'1) Set out the same mixed pieces from the colour sort and announce the change: today we sort by shape — circles here, squares there.\n2) Sort one of each yourself with the question spoken every time: is it round? Then it is a circle. Corners? Square. The rule, made audible.\n3) Hand over the next piece and let your toddler ask and answer in their own way — a hover, a turn-over, a tap, then a drop.\n4) When the piles stand finished, point at both and say the big idea plainly: the same toys, sorted a new way. Same pieces — different rule.\n5) Swap rules once more if the mood holds — by size, by what rolls — then mix everything back and let the day have its toys returned.',
 say:'"New rule — shapes today! Circles here, squares there. Is this one round? Circle — here it goes. Does this one have corners? Square! Look — same toys, sorted a different way. You did that!"',
 notice:'The card shows circles and squares for a sorting-by-shape activity — one family of round shapes, one family of cornered shapes, waiting for their teams. Ask your toddler to tap a circle, then a square, before the piles begin.',
 ask:'"Is this one round or cornered?" "Where do the circles go?" "Can we sort them a new way?" "What rule is today?"',
 childSay:'Expect the rule to wobble: a square dropped with the circles, retrieved, then dropped right. The retrieve is the milestone — nobody mentions the wobble, everybody celebrates the second try.',
 real:'Two-bowl lunches sort by shape for free: round cucumber coins one bowl, square cheese slices the other. Blocks back in the box by shape is tidy-up doing a lesson’s job.',
 play:'Rule dance: sort by shape, then colour, then shape again — the same handful of pieces, three rules, one rug. Announce each rule change like a starting whistle and watch the brain change gear.',
 check:'Ask for the circles only, from a mixed line of six pieces. All circles collected passes. A square handed over with a sorry face passes too — the checking glance before the drop is shape knowledge in action.',
 tips:'One rule at a time keeps the game honest: announce it, play it, then change it with a whistle and a grin. If your toddler invents a rule of their own — soft things here — that is the lesson graduating with honours.',
 safety:'Shape-sorting pieces obey the toilet-roll rule: too big to swallow, too light to hurt. The game ends when pieces start flying — rule-switching includes switching to thrown, and grown-ups call that ending.'},
'fruit-together':{
 title:'Fruit Together',one:'Apple and banana on one side, a car and a shoe sneaking in — which ones belong together? The first group question.',
 seoTitle:'Fruit Together Lesson for 2-Year-Olds — How to Teach Matching and Sorting (Class 10)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for 2-year-olds: find the fruit among the impostors, name the group out loud, and sort the fruit bowl for real afterwards.',
 intro:'This lesson asks the deck’s first group question: of four pictured things, which two go together? The fruit do — and the odd pair makes the answer fun to find.',
 what:'A group is things that belong together by kind. Apples and bananas are both fruit — a car and a shoe are both… not. The odd pair is not a mistake; it is the question.',
 mean:'Grouping jumps from this-matches-that to a rule that covers many things at once. Fruit is the friendliest category going: eaten daily, held daily, named at the table without anyone trying.',
 know:'The impostors are the teaching: naming what does not belong — the car does not grow on trees! — proves the category from the outside. Laugh at the shoe; the laugh is the lesson landing.',
 why:'The fruit bowl reruns this card at every meal, and shopping reruns it in the aisles. A category your toddler already eats needs no new vocabulary — just a name said out loud.',
 dev:'Category forming, odd-one noticing, and the two-sided thinking of belongs and does-not-belong — the root of every later is-it-alive, is-it-red, does-it-fit conversation.',
 vocab:'fruit, group, together, apple, banana, belong',
 teach:'1) Show the card and name all four things first, one point each: an apple, a banana, a car, a shoe. No sorting yet — just the roll call.\n2) Ask the question slowly: which two go together? Then let the looking happen in silence if it wants to.\n3) Confirm with the group name: the apple and the banana are fruit — fruit go together! Trace a circle around the pair with one finger.\n4) Give the odd pair their moment: a car is for driving, a shoe is for wearing — neither one grows on a tree. The outsides prove the inside of the group.\n5) Rerun it at the real fruit bowl: hand your toddler three things, two fruit and one not, and let the fruit get found and named as a group.',
 say:'"Four things! An apple, a banana, a car, a shoe. Which two go together? The apple and the banana — fruit! Fruit together. Can a car grow on a tree? No! Silly car — fruit belong together."',
 notice:'The card shows an apple, a banana, a car and a shoe for a grouping activity — two fruit and two everyday things that are not fruit. Ask your toddler to name all four, then point at the two that belong together.',
 ask:'"Which two go together?" "Where is the fruit?" "Does the shoe belong?" "What do we do with a car?"',
 childSay:'A two-finger grab of the fruit pair, or a push of the car and shoe to one side — clearing the outsiders is a full answer. Fruit said as foo or boo with the right point is the category forming.',
 real:'The shopping basket is a grouping game your toddler can run: fruit in the bag, boxes in the trolley. At home, the fruit bowl asks its question fresh every single day.',
 play:'Bowl check: hand over three things from the kitchen — two fruit, one not — and ask for the fruit. Then reverse the job for laughs: feed the car? Wear the banana? Wrong uses are the funny proof that groups are real.',
 check:'Next snack, offer fruit and one stranger on the same plate and ask what goes together. Fruit gathered and stranger refused — or stranger cheerfully named as not-fruit — both pass with top marks.',
 tips:'Name the group, then name the outsiders: both halves teach. If your toddler insists the car goes with the fruit because they are both red, say the thinking back warmly — then ask what the car is for.',
 safety:'Kitchen grouping games use real food and real objects: fruit gets washed, and nothing smaller than a golf ball joins the plate. The shoe stays on the floor where shoes belong — the game agrees.'},
'animals-together':{
 title:'Animals Together',one:'A cat and a dog with a cup and a ball for company — which two are the animals? Grouping meets living things.',
 seoTitle:'Animals Together Lesson for 2-Year-Olds — How to Teach Matching and Sorting (Class 10)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for 2-year-olds: gather the animals on the card, name the group, and sort real toys into animals and everything else.',
 intro:'This lesson points the grouping skill at living things: two animals sit among two everyday objects, and your toddler’s job is to gather the creatures home.',
 what:'Animals are the things that move, eat and make noise — a cat and a dog are both animals, and a cup and a ball are both not. The category is big, old and full of friends.',
 mean:'Living versus not-living is one of the deepest sorts there is, and toddlers begin it with the animals they already love. Every pet, bird and book creature feeds the same growing group.',
 know:'Accept group logic in all its toddler forms: if the ball joins the animals because it is round and bouncy, say the thinking back, then ask what the cat can do that the ball cannot.',
 why:'Animals fill the day — pets, parks, pages, puddle birds. A group this crowded gets rehearsed from every direction without the card ever leaving the shelf.',
 dev:'Category building with living things, the belongs and does-not-belong contrast, and sound-and-motion proof — animals move and make noise, and cups do neither.',
 vocab:'animals, cat, dog, together, group, alive',
 teach:'1) Show the card and roll-call all four: a cat, a dog, a cup, a ball. One name, one point, no rushing.\n2) Ask the big question: which two are the animals? Give the looking all the time it needs.\n3) Gather the animals with your finger and name the group: cat and dog — animals! Animals go together.\n4) Prove the category with what animals do: the cat moves, the dog says woof. Does the cup say woof? Does the ball walk? The outsiders stay funny while the inside gets firm.\n5) Rerun on the rug: sort real toys into animals and everything else, two corners of the rug, and let the animal corner grow loud with all its sounds.',
 say:'"Who is on this card? A cat, a dog, a cup, a ball. Which two are the animals? The cat and the dog! Animals together — because animals move and make sounds. Does a cup say woof? Nooo!"',
 notice:'The card shows a cat, a dog, a cup and a ball for a grouping activity — two animals and two things that are not animals. Ask your toddler to point at the animals, then at each thing that cannot move by itself.',
 ask:'"Which ones are the animals?" "What does the dog say?" "Does the cup move?" "Where are your toy animals?"',
 childSay:'The animal corner tends to get loud: woofs and meows are the group being celebrated. A toddler who herds the cup in with the cat is testing the border — the border conversation is the lesson.',
 real:'Every dog on a lead, pigeon on the path and cat on a wall keeps the group growing. Point, name, and add the magic word: animal — the category collects them all.',
 play:'Animal corner: two sides of the rug, toys sorted by alive and not. Then visit the animal side with all the sounds and movements — the corner that moves and speaks wins the game every time.',
 check:'Ask for the animals from a mixed row of four toys. Animals gathered to one side passes outright. A ball gathered too, with woof sounds attached, passes as thinking-in-progress — say what the ball cannot do and let it re-home.',
 tips:'Keep the category loose and lively: animals move, eat and make sounds is enough definition for two. The fine print of biology can wait for school — the warm certainty that animals are their own kind cannot.',
 safety:'Toy animal sorts stay off the stairs and away from radiators; the two-corners game needs floor space, not furniture. Real animals stay behind owner rules — the sorting is for toys and pictures only.'},
'odd-one-out':{
 title:'The Odd One Out',one:'Three bananas and one apple walk onto a card — and the one that breaks the pattern is the whole puzzle.',
 seoTitle:'The Odd One Out Lesson for 2-Year-Olds — How to Teach Matching and Sorting (Class 10)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for 2-year-olds: spot the fruit that does not belong, say different out loud, and play the odd-one game with real tableware.',
 intro:'This lesson is the deck’s grand finale: three things that match and one that does not, and your toddler gets to find the stranger and say so.',
 what:'The odd one out is the thing that breaks the pattern. Three bananas make a row; the apple does not belong — and spotting that break is a two-year-old thinking milestone.',
 mean:'This puzzle stacks two skills: seeing what the group is, then testing each member against it. The stranger only looks strange once the pattern is held in mind — that holding is the growing part.',
 know:'Hard is honest: this is the trickiest card in the deck, and wrong grabs are part of the path. Shrink the game to real objects when the card stalls, and grow it back when the grin returns.',
 why:'Odd-one-out is dinner-table gold: three spoons and one cup, three crackers and one apple. The pattern needs nothing but the table setting, rerun nightly for free.',
 dev:'Pattern spotting, holding a rule while checking members against it, and different used as a verdict — the fullest thinking workout the deck offers.',
 vocab:'different, odd, one, bananas, apple, belong',
 teach:'1) Show the card and name the row in order, one point each: banana, banana, banana… apple. Let the rhythm break all by itself at the end.\n2) Ask the question: which one is different? Then wait through the thinking — the pause is where the comparing happens.\n3) Confirm the find with the pattern said out loud: three bananas, and one apple — the apple is different! Trace from banana to banana, then stop at the apple.\n4) Celebrate the oddity, not a win: the apple is not wrong to be there — it is the interesting one. Different is a discovery, not a mistake.\n5) Rerun it small and real: three spoons and one cup at lunch, and let your toddler hand you the stranger. Tables make friendly versions of tricky cards.',
 say:'"Banana, banana, banana… apple! Wait — which one is different? Look again… the apple! Three bananas, one apple. The apple is the odd one out! Can you say different?"',
 notice:'The card shows three bananas and one apple for an odd-one-out activity — three matching fruit and one that breaks the row. Ask your toddler to tap each banana in turn, then find the fruit that is not a banana.',
 ask:'"Which one is different?" "Are they all bananas?" "What is this one?" "Can you find the one that does not belong?"',
 childSay:'A slow point that visits each fruit before landing, or a snatch of the apple held up like evidence — both are answers. Different said as diff-rent is the word of the month; keep saying it back.',
 real:'Table settings run this puzzle nightly: three spoons and one fork, three cups and one plate. Hand over the job of fetching the odd one and the thinking becomes a chore anyone would volunteer for.',
 play:'Stranger soup: line up four snacks, three the same and one odd, and let your toddler evict the stranger with a flourish. Then swap jobs — you close your eyes, they build the row, and you get to be wrong on cue.',
 check:'Ask cold at the next tidy-up: line up three books and one bear and request the different one. A clean pick passes. A pick of the bear with reasons attached — bear is soft! — passes best of all.',
 tips:'If the card stalls, shrink it: two of one thing, one of another, on the table. Tricky cards grow back — the trick is ending every round before the wobble, never after.',
 safety:'Tableware versions use toddler-safe pieces — no knives in the sorting game, ever. Snack rows sit on the plate, not the floor, and stay bigger than a toilet-roll tube right up until they are eaten.'}
};
